Sleep deprivation costs the US economy $411 billion in lost productivity annually (RAND Corporation, 2016); remote workers who eliminate commutes sleep 13 minutes more per night on average, meaningfully improving their cognitive function.
Burnout — a $322 billion annual cost globally according to Gallup — is significantly lower in employees who report adequate work-life balance; remote flexibility is one of the most effective structural interventions.
The 'always on' risk of remote work is real but addressable through clear boundaries; organisations with defined disconnect policies see the productivity gains without the burnout costs.
Research by Arlie Hochschild and others shows that reducing the second shift burden (commute + household logistics) disproportionately benefits working parents and women, broadening access to high-performance careers.
Retention improves dramatically with remote flexibility: LinkedIn data shows remote job postings receive 4× as many applications as equivalent on-site roles, reducing costly turnover.
